{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the last count of the County of Tripoli. He was born around 1152 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hethum of Edessa. \n\nHis early life and succession are not well documented however it is believed he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1187. His mother Hethum of Edessa was the daughter of Constantine of Baberon and the sister of Thoros III of Armenia. \n\n*Early Life and Succession*\n\n*Early Life and Succession* is not well documented however it is believed he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1187. He was married to Stephanie of Milly and then to Isabelle of Giblet. \n\n*Relationships with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\n\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1187 he paid homage to Saladin after the Battle of Hattin and he also provided troops to Saladin during the Siege of Acre in 1189. However in 1191 he allied with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and participated in the Third Crusade. \n\n*Relationships with the Kingdom of Armenia*\n\nRaymond III also had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Armenia. He married his sister Isabelle to Bohemond III of Antioch but this alliance was short lived as Bohemond III divorced Isabelle and married the daughter of Constantine of Baberon. \n\nIn 1193 Raymond III married Isabelle of Giblet and had two sons with her. However Isabelle died in 1201 and Raymond III married again to Alice of Armenia and had two more sons with her. Raymond III died in 1187 was incorrect and he actually died in 1188 was incorrect as well. He actually died in 1197.\n\nThe County of Tripoli was eventually inherited by his son Raymond-Roupen and later by his grandson Bohemond IV of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.021772763}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00021634146}, {"token": " III", "logprob"